#: ../content/index.rst:74
msgid "Basic Configuration (Both Provider and Invoker)"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:76
msgid "Your system-administrator should configure one (or more) OAuth2 Application(s) with their relevant OIDC provider and give you the following configuration details:"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:78
msgid "the well-known provider metadata discovery url as described in the `openid spec <https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-discovery-1_0.html#ProviderConfigurationRequest>`__  Example: '\\https://keycloak:8443/realms/master/.well-known/openid-configuration'"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:79
msgid "``client_id`` and ``client_secret`` parameters for your OAuth2 Application"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:80
msgid "the ``UID`` identifier of an OAuth2 JWT token claim that can be mapped to an Otobo UserLogin identifier. Usual values include 'sub', 'email', or 'uid'. Use 'sub' if in doubt."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:82
msgid "If your planning to use functional accounts (aka System Users) to authorize outgoing API calls triggered by Otobo:"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:84
msgid "the ``grant_type`` which will be one of: 'client_credentials' or 'password' or 'authorization_code'. if grant_type is 'password', you will also get username/password credentials"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:85
msgid "optionally, a set of ``scope`` parameters to use. When using OIDC this likely will include 'openid'"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:86
msgid "optionally, a set of ``resource`` ids to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:88
msgid "**Important**"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:90
msgid "If you are using the 'authorization_code' flow, which will redirect your browser to the Provider login page to authenticate, the OIDC Provider will redirect you back to your Otobo instance when the authentication was successful. For this to work the 'redirect_uri' that points back to you Otobo instance must be an allowed redirect_uri configured for your OAuth2 OIDC application. The generated URL will be ``http(s)://your-otobo.fqdn/otobo/index.pl?Action=AdminOAuthTokenStore&Subaction=OAuth'``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:94
msgid "Note this is in addition to the Login redirect_uri which usually is ``http(s)://your-otobo.fqdn/otobo/index.pl?Action=Login'``."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:97
msgid "Some Provider allow simple wildcards in the redirect_uri setting, like ``http(s)://your-otobo.fqdn/otobo/index.pl*`` - Please consult your OIDC Provider documentation for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:101
msgid "Otobo as Provider"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:104
msgid "Basic OpenID Connect Provider Configuration in Otobo"
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:106
msgid "For incoming OAuth2 API calls, Otobo has to authenticate the incoming OAuth2 Tokens and map them to an Otobo user. For API calls this works using the same Authentication Modules ('AuthModule' in Config.pm) as used for Login via OpenID Connect. If you already using Otobo for OIDC based login, you should already have a valid OIDC configuration like from the example above and **you are all set**."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:110
msgid "Otherwise, in case you are not using OIDC for Login, but still want to use OIDC for API calls, or if you want to use a OIDC provider for API calls different from your default OIDC configuration used for login, add an additional AuthModule for OIDC."
msgstr ""

#: ../content/index.rst:113
msgid "Refer to the examples for configuring OIDC that come with your Otobo installation. The examples are in Kernel/Config/Defaults.pm line 518ff, and this needs to be configured in ``Config.pm``."
msgstr ""

